    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d51">

                <pb facs="0060" n="48" xml:id="pg48"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">810.20 Defense/163⅜ Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Brazil</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Caffery</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Rio de
                                Janeiro</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1940-06-14T00:00:00-03:00">June 14,
                        1940—midnight</date>.<lb/>[Received June 15—5:30 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>281. For Under Secretary. My 279, June 14, 7 p.m.<anchor n="62" xml:id="d51-f1"/>
                    In this armament matter the Brazilian military authorities since July speak very
                    highly of General Marshall and in no way blame him for the failure of arms to
                    come from the United States. They understand our legislative difficulties.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Caffery</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
